# Scheduled maintenance.
#
# Doctrine: doctrine/operations/scheduled-maintenance-design.md
#
# Rules encoded in the layout below:
#
#   * NOTHING STARTS AT :00. Jobs that all fire on the hour contend for CPU,
#     memory, disk, and network at the same moment — on a small board that is
#     enough to trigger a memory event and get something important killed.
#
#   * QUIET ON SUCCESS. A job with nothing to report produces nothing. Every job
#     here therefore pushes a heartbeat on completion, and the uptime monitor
#     alerts on the heartbeat NOT ARRIVING. Without that, a job that never runs
#     is indistinguishable from a job with nothing to do.
#
#   * BOUNDED. `timeout` on everything. An unbounded scheduled job is how one
#     hung request becomes a permanently held lock.
#
#   * NON-OVERLAPPING. `flock -n` so a slow run is skipped rather than doubled.
#     The lock is on a file, so a crashed run does not block every future one.
#
#   * NEVER SILENTLY SKIP. A job whose target is unreachable must record that.
#     Unreachable is unknown, and unknown must be visible — it is not healthy.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
LAB_HOME={{ lab.home_dir }}

# --- Self-healing ------------------------------------------------------------
# Watchdogs run as systemd timers, not cron. See watchdogs/systemd/.
# Timers give per-instance cadence, boot delay, and randomized spread, none of
# which cron does well.

# --- Freshness and reconciliation -------------------------------------------
# Hourly. Compares intended state against observed state and corrects the
# difference. This is what catches an edge-triggered automation whose trigger
# was missed — without it, nothing ever retries and the system sits in a state
# nobody intended.
7 * * * * flock -n /tmp/lab-reconcile.lock timeout 300 ${LAB_HOME}/automation/reconcile.sh

# --- Daily ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Log rotation and cache trimming. Cheap, and prevents the slow disk-fill that
# eventually presents as an unrelated service failing.
23 4 * * * flock -n /tmp/lab-trim.lock timeout 600 ${LAB_HOME}/automation/trim.sh

# --- Weekly -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Backup. Deliberately NOT adjacent to the image refresh below — both are
# I/O-heavy and one of them would be starved.
{{ backup.schedule }} flock -n /tmp/lab-backup.lock timeout 7200 ${LAB_HOME}/automation/backup.sh

# Per-service health pass. Files one record per service that needs work, and
# NOTHING when everything is fine.
{{ maintenance.service_schedule }} flock -n /tmp/lab-health.lock timeout 1800 ${LAB_HOME}/automation/health-pass.sh

# --- Monthly ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Host packages, container image refresh, image and volume pruning.
# Long, disruptive, and the most likely to need a rollback — so it runs when
# nobody is using the house, and it backs up first.
{{ maintenance.host_schedule }} flock -n /tmp/lab-maint.lock timeout 10800 ${LAB_HOME}/automation/host-maintenance.sh

# Restore verification. An unverified backup is a belief, not a backup — this
# restores to a scratch location and checks the result. If you only adopt one
# line from this file, adopt this one.
41 5 1 * * flock -n /tmp/lab-restore.lock timeout 3600 ${LAB_HOME}/automation/verify-restore.sh
